From 7dfd58e7bee6b1e7fab2ca87de0fb848c10fd16d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthony Ruhier <aruhier@mailbox.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 22:53:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] platform: arm64: qcom-hamoa-ec: don't fail probe on bad
 thermal capabilities

Since commit 1b04f556e842 ("platform: arm64: qcom-hamoa-ec: reject
incomplete responses"), qcom_ec_read() rejects responses whose byte
count doesn't match the expected length. The Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x EC
returns such a response to EC_THERMAL_CAP_CMD, so the probe now fails
with -EINVAL.

Without a bound driver the EC is never notified of modern standby
entry/exit, which breaks s2idle. Warn instead and carry on as if no fan
was reported, which is what used to happen before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/platform/arm64/qcom-hamoa-ec.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/arm64/qcom-hamoa-ec.c b/drivers/platform/arm64/qcom-hamoa-ec.c
index 4d2ad042a7f84..26a197dcce3e2 100644
--- a/drivers/platform/arm64/qcom-hamoa-ec.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/arm64/qcom-hamoa-ec.c
@@ -372,9 +372,14 @@ static int qcom_ec_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Failed to enable SCI events\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * Some EC firmwares (e.g. Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x) return a malformed
+	 * thermal capabilities response. Don't fail the probe for it, the EC
+	 * still needs to be notified of modern standby entry/exit.
+	 */
 	ret = qcom_ec_thermal_capabilities(dev);
 	if (ret < 0)
-		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Failed to read thermal capabilities\n");
+		dev_warn(dev, "Failed to read thermal capabilities: %d\n", ret);
 
 	if (ec->thermal_cap.fan_cnt == 0) {
 		dev_warn(dev, FW_BUG "Failed to get fan count, firmware update required\n");
